<!-- ============================================================
     Orexin Ransomware: Windows SQL Server Ransomware
     MITRE ATT&amp;CK Techniques:
       T1490     - Inhibit System Recovery (TA0040 Impact)
       T1489     - Service Stop (TA0040 Impact)
       T1486     - Data Encrypted for Impact (TA0040 Impact)
       T1059.003 - Command and Scripting Interpreter: Windows Command Shell (TA0002)
       T1110.001 - Brute Force: Password Guessing (TA0006 Credential Access)
     Rule IDs: 104192-104199
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team, ESKA-1077)
     Date: 2026-11-02
     Source: CYFIRMA Weekly Intelligence Report, July 24, 2026

     Threat Summary:

     Orexin Ransomware is a newly documented Windows ransomware strain
     observed in underground forums on July 24, 2026, specifically targeting
     SQL Server database servers. Key attack chain:

       1. Initial access via RDP brute-force or exposed SQL Server port
       2. Privilege escalation via token impersonation
       3. SQL Server services terminated to release DB file locks (T1489):
            MSSQLSERVER, SQLSERVERAGENT, MSSQLFDLauncher, SQLBrowser
       4. Shadow copies deleted (T1490):
            vssadmin delete shadows /all /quiet
       5. Boot recovery disabled (T1490):
            bcdedit /set {default} recoveryenabled No
       6. SQL database and backup files encrypted recursively (T1486):
            .mdf, .ldf, .bak, .sql files renamed with .orexin extension
            double-extension pattern: database.mdf.orexin, data.sql.orexin
       7. Ransom note dropped: OREXIN-README.txt on each encrypted host

     Log source prerequisites:
       - Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate)  - Wazuh SID 61603
         Required for rules 104192 (vssadmin), 104193 (bcdedit),
         104194 (net stop SQL), 104195 (sc.exe stop SQL)
       - Sysmon EventID 11 (FileCreate)    - Wazuh SID 61644
         Required for rules 104196 (.orexin extension), 104197 (ransom note)
       - Sysmon EventID 11 frequency correlation:
         Rule 104198 correlates rule 104196 for mass encryption detection.
       - Orexin group correlation:
         Rule 104199 fires on 3+ distinct Orexin indicators within 10 minutes.

     Severity tiers:
       11 - SQL Server service stopped via net stop or sc.exe (may FP in maintenance)
       12 - vssadmin.exe shadow copy deletion
       12 - bcdedit.exe recovery mode disabled
       12 - .orexin file extension created (base for frequency rule 104198)
       14 - Mass .orexin file creation 10+ files in 60s (active encryption)
       15 - OREXIN-README.txt ransom note created (confirmed detonation)
       15 - 3+ Orexin indicators within 10 minutes (correlated attack confirmed)

     References:
       CYFIRMA Weekly Intelligence Report, July 24, 2026
       MITRE ATT&amp;CK T1486: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1486/
       MITRE ATT&amp;CK T1490: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1490/
       MITRE ATT&amp;CK T1489: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1489/
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="threat_intel,impact,defense_evasion,windows,orexin_ransomware,ransomware,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Sysmon EventID 1: vssadmin.exe deleting shadow copies (T1490)
       Orexin Ransomware deletes all VSS shadow copies immediately before
       encryption using: vssadmin delete shadows /all /quiet
       This prevents Windows System Restore and volume snapshot-based recovery.
       vssadmin with delete shadows arguments has no legitimate routine use
       in production environments; any occurrence warrants investigation.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104192" level="12">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\vssadmin\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)delete\s+shadows</field>
    <description>Orexin Ransomware: vssadmin.exe delete shadows executed by $(win.eventdata.user) on $(win.eventdata.computer) - shadow copy deletion pre-encryption anti-recovery step (T1490)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1490</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ransomware,backup_deletion,orexin_ransomware,shadow_copy,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Sysmon EventID 1: bcdedit.exe disabling Windows recovery (T1490)
       Orexin Ransomware disables boot-time recovery before encryption:
         bcdedit /set {default} recoveryenabled No
       This prevents the victim from booting into recovery mode to restore
       encrypted files. Disabling recovery via bcdedit is a known ransomware
       TTP used by Orexin and other families (INC, BlackCat, LockBit).
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104193" level="12">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\bcdedit\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)recoveryenabled\s+no</field>
    <description>Orexin Ransomware: bcdedit.exe disabling Windows recovery mode on $(win.eventdata.computer) - boot recovery prevention before ransomware encryption (T1490)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1490</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ransomware,backup_deletion,orexin_ransomware,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Sysmon EventID 1: net.exe stopping SQL Server services (T1489)
       Orexin Ransomware terminates SQL Server services before encryption
       to release file locks on .mdf, .ldf, and .bak database files.
       Targeted service names include: MSSQLSERVER, SQLSERVERAGENT,
       MSSQLFDLauncher, SQLBrowser, ReportServer, and sql-prefixed variants.
       Note: legitimate DBA maintenance windows may generate false positives;
       correlate with concurrent vssadmin or bcdedit activity.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104194" level="11">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\net1?\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)stop\s+(MSSQL|MSSQLSERVER|SQLAgent|SQLBrowser|SQLServer|ReportServer|MSSQLFDLauncher|sql)</field>
    <description>Orexin Ransomware: net.exe stopped SQL Server service as $(win.eventdata.user) on $(win.eventdata.computer) - pre-encryption DB lock release; Orexin kills SQL services to access and encrypt .mdf/.ldf/.bak database files (T1489)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1489</id>
      <id>T1486</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ransomware,service_stop,orexin_ransomware,sql_server,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Sysmon EventID 1: sc.exe stopping SQL Server services (T1489)
       Orexin Ransomware may use sc.exe (Service Control Manager CLI) as
       an alternative to net stop for terminating SQL Server services.
       The sc.exe stop subcommand against any MSSQL or SQL-prefixed service
       is functionally identical to net stop and indicates the same
       pre-encryption DB lock-release technique.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104195" level="11">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\sc\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)stop\s+(MSSQL|MSSQLSERVER|SQLAgent|SQLBrowser|SQLServer|ReportServer|MSSQLFDLauncher|sql)</field>
    <description>Orexin Ransomware: sc.exe stopped SQL Server service as $(win.eventdata.user) on $(win.eventdata.computer) - pre-encryption DB lock release via Service Control Manager; Orexin terminates SQL services before encrypting database files (T1489)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1489</id>
      <id>T1486</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ransomware,service_stop,orexin_ransomware,sql_server,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Sysmon EventID 11: .orexin file extension created (base rule, T1486)
       Orexin Ransomware appends .orexin to each encrypted file, producing
       a double-extension pattern: database.mdf.orexin, archive.bak.orexin.
       This extension does not exist in any legitimate Windows application.
       Any file creation with .orexin extension is a high-confidence indicator
       of active Orexin ransomware encryption. This rule serves as the base
       event for frequency correlation rule 104198.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104196" level="12">
    <if_sid>61644</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetFilename" type="pcre2">(?i)\.orexin$</field>
    <description>Orexin Ransomware: file with .orexin extension created at $(win.eventdata.targetFilename) on $(win.eventdata.computer) - active file encryption indicator; Orexin appends .orexin to each encrypted SQL database file (T1486)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1486</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ransomware,file_creation,orexin_ransomware,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Sysmon EventID 11: OREXIN-README.txt ransom note created (CRITICAL)
       Orexin Ransomware drops its ransom note as OREXIN-README.txt on
       every encrypted host after completing the encryption phase. This
       filename is unique to Orexin and does not appear in any legitimate
       application. Its creation anywhere on the filesystem is a near-certain
       indicator of completed Orexin ransomware detonation. Immediate incident
       response is required: isolate host, preserve forensic evidence, initiate
       recovery from clean offline backups.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104197" level="15">
    <if_sid>61644</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetFilename" type="pcre2">(?i)OREXIN-README\.txt$</field>
    <description>Orexin Ransomware: CRITICAL - OREXIN-README.txt ransom note created at $(win.eventdata.targetFilename) on $(win.eventdata.computer) - OREXIN RANSOMWARE ENCRYPTION COMPLETE; isolate host immediately and initiate IR (T1486)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1486</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ransomware_detonation,impact,orexin_ransomware,critical,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Frequency: Mass .orexin file creation (active encryption in progress)
       When 10 or more .orexin files are created within 60 seconds, Orexin
       ransomware is actively bulk-encrypting the filesystem. This frequency
       rule correlates base rule 104196 to confirm mass encryption, raising
       the alert to CRITICAL severity. The 10-file / 60-second threshold is
       calibrated against observed ransomware encryption throughput on SQL
       Server workloads (INC, Spirals baselines) to minimize false positives.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104198" level="14" frequency="10" timeframe="60">
    <if_matched_sid>104196</if_matched_sid>
    <description>Orexin Ransomware: mass .orexin file creation detected - 10 or more encrypted files in 60 seconds on $(win.eventdata.computer) - ACTIVE RANSOMWARE ENCRYPTION IN PROGRESS; contain host immediately (T1486)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1486</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ransomware_detonation,impact,orexin_ransomware,critical,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Correlation: 3 or more Orexin indicators within 10 minutes
       When 3 or more distinct Orexin ransomware indicators fire within
       600 seconds, this confirms an active multi-stage Orexin attack.
       This rule correlates shadow copy deletion (104192), recovery mode
       disabling (104193), SQL service termination (104194-104195), and
       file encryption (104196-104197) into a high-confidence attack
       confirmation alert. At this severity, the attack chain is already
       well underway; immediate containment is required.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104199" level="15" frequency="3" timeframe="600">
    <if_matched_group>orexin_ransomware</if_matched_group>
    <description>Orexin Ransomware: CRITICAL - 3 or more Orexin attack indicators within 10 minutes on $(win.eventdata.computer) - active multi-stage Orexin ransomware attack confirmed; initiate IR immediately (T1486, T1490, T1489)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1486</id>
      <id>T1490</id>
      <id>T1489</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ransomware_detonation,impact,orexin_ransomware,critical,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
